00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman here  again to talk about language learning  and i'm going to  focus today on compelling input ci  which i consider to be  a revolution in language learning a  revolution not because  it's totally new in the sense that i  think that's always how people have  learned languages but it's a revolution  in the sense that  language instruction language teachers  are increasingly recognizing that we  learn the language through input  hopefully compelling input  and that a lot of the  specific sort of language instruction  testing  grammar explanation is  rather  is significantly less important  that the time we spend on that  activity is usually better spent  with compelling input  now in that regard  tomorrow i'm going to portland oregon or  near portland oregon where stephen  krashen and benico mason  are running a clinic on story listening  they have these clinics uh in various  places in the united states perhaps  elsewhere  and they work with teachers  and explain and talk about different  techniques for  using story listening  as the sort of core  instructional technique  as opposed to  explanations drills and testing  i don't know much more about it than  that i will know much more after  tomorrow  but  it's people like particularly stephen  krashen and beniko mason who are the  revolutionaries who have introduced this  simple concept of compelling input and i  know that in my own case i have used  compelling input to learn languages if  you look behind me you see books  and you know cds and stuff and i have  immersed myself in all kinds of  compelling input in order to learn  languages and have spent relatively  little time on  grammar explanations it doesn't mean i  have spent no time on grammar  explanations i do review them the  grammar rules with no intention of  remembering what i see  i review you know declension tables with  no intention of remembering what i see  there but i do find that it helps to  make me  a little more alert  helps me to get more out of my input  activities  and apparently there's a story that abe  lincoln said if i were going to cut down  a tree i would spend 80 percent of my  time sharpening the axe so some of these  materials some of these activities  rather  can help you make can help make you more  alert more observant i notice in my  arabic for example after each lesson i  go through the words and phrases in fact  i deliberately say certain phrases and  then i review them using a dictation so  i'm forced to write out in arabic what i  hear  i this does slow me down in terms of  acquiring new words but on the other  hand it helps me read better because  with arabic a major problem is the  writing system i'm still confusing  letters uh arabic letters and so forth  and so on  so compelling input one other thing with  regard to input i just did a 27-minute  interview with oli richards who is a  great  polyglot and has a following and i'm  going to put links to his blog and his  website here  and oli has produced graded readers  in a variety of languages and oli who  speaks who is fluent in a lot of  languages including three romance  languages has decided to  spend two to three months focusing on  italian very much input-based and he's  going to keep a record of his activities  and progress he's going to do this with  the link and we talk about this in a  video and the video will be put up on  this channel on thursday  so while i mention oli and i mentioned  graded readers i want to touch a bit on  the subject of compelling input now  tomorrow or the following day  when i meet with stephen krashen and and  benico mason and others  we will talk about compelling input one  issue with compelling input is when you  start in a language and people often ask  me how do you start listening how do you  start reading you don't know anything  obviously you have to start with simple  material you have to start with simple  material where you have both audio and  text  i do this of course using link because  then i can look words up and i can  review them and and then listen and if i  you know create playlists of my lessons  then the playlists track how many hours  i'm listening and so there are a lot of  statistics there that remind me that i  am progressing in the language even  though at times i feel frustrated that i  think i'm not making any great progress  so there is i think there are three  stages of  input  the first stage is these simple  materials like our many stories which  are not that simple but  i think they're accessible for most  people at a very early stage if not the  absolute beginner stage i'm using them  as a beginner in arabic  that that this is material that is not  authentic  it's not tremendously compelling it  talks about everyday situations in life  uses you know high frequency verbs and  there's a lot of repetition but because  we are struggling to get a toehold on  the language and we're struggling to  notice and remember words and phrases to  that extent it's compelling i find  because i am motivated to learn the  language but very soon you want to move  into something where the actual content  is of itself interesting  so oli for example has these graded  readers  people may invent stories children's  stories the difficulty there is that  people's interests vary like i would be  interested in a graded reader for  example in arabic on arabic history i'm  not so interested in a graded reader  taken from a thousand and one nights for  example i just i'm less interested in  that i would be interested in  sort of relatively easy conversation  natural conversation which is then  transcribed because natural conversation  tends to use  more high frequency words so all of  those things would be the kind of graded  material that i'm interested in other  people might be interested in children's  stories folk tales or whatever so we  have the the beginner material with a  lot of repetition we have graded  material which it's a little more  difficult to find something that suits a  wide variety of tastes and then we have  the sort of genuine authentic material  and there we can find it on the internet  and audio for example il naratore who  produces wonderful italian uh ebooks  naratorepuntocom.com and he puts out  audio ebooks so you can take the ebook  import it into link listen to the audio  so  to me i see compelling input as  consisting of the beginner material a  lot of repetition  intermediate material which is  lower sort of or more higher percentage  of high frequency vocabulary and then  the genuinely authentic compelling input  so i'm very interested in finding out  with these teachers in portland and  stephen krashen and beniko mason how  they deal with these sort of stages of  compelling input because compelling has  also to be comprehensible  uh  one final point here i'm interested in  meeting with teachers  and  we at link we want to get more involved  with groups like a classroom group or  other groups how do you  adapt link  to a classroom situation or to some  other group learning situation what are  the tasks that you assign your learners  uh  that they bring to the classroom or to  the group discussion and and what do  they do then in the classroom and how  does that relate to their  uh link learning activity  and uh we have one uh person in spain  antonio who is working with us to try to  uh  make more schools universities and other  groups in spain aware of link and how it  can be tremendously beneficial to their  language learning  part of it is to explain the power of  compelling input i mean links as link is  is a facilitator  a system that makes compelling input  more accessible more quickly and helps  you retain vocabulary  so antonio  has asked me asked me to do a video  introducing link and i did this in  spanish and so after this video you'll  be able to listen to uh my video in  spanish i apologize there are no  subtitles  we also have a collaborator constantine  who is in kiev and who is an online  tutor to russian speakers wherever they  might be located  he went to bratislava to the polyglot  conference and spoke about how he uses  link in his online tutoring  activity but he is also looking at ways  in ukraine where he lives and perhaps  elsewhere in the russian speaking world  to  look at how link can be used as part of  a group or classroom based learning  experience so those are kinds of the the  kind of the directions that we are  heading into  and so if you understand spanish you can  listen to my spanish video  and thursday you'll hear my discussion  with oli and we will have an opportunity  to follow  all his progress  alright then thank you for listening bye  for now  [Music]  you

